\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces End-to-end pipeline: raw CT volume $\rightarrow $ HU windowing $[-175, 250]$ $\rightarrow $ per-slice resize to $256\times 256$ $\rightarrow $ Attention U-Net inference $\rightarrow $ multi-organ segmentation mask (13 organ classes).}}{2}{figure.caption.5}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Per-organ voxel frequency in the FLARE22 training set. The liver contributes the largest share of foreground voxels; adrenal glands, esophagus, and duodenum each represent less than 1\% of foreground.}}{10}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Axial CT slice from validation case FLARE22\_Tr\_0007 (left) and the corresponding ground-truth organ label overlay (right). Each colour represents one of the 13 annotated organs. HU intensities are windowed to $[-175, 250]$ prior to display.}}{11}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Online augmentation applied during training. From left to right: original slice, horizontal flip, vertical flip, and brightness shift of $+30$~HU. All transforms are applied on-the-fly so the model never sees the same transformed slice twice.}}{13}{figure.caption.11}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Attention U-Net architecture used in this project. The encoder (left) progressively downsamples through four blocks; the decoder (right) upsamples and fuses encoder features via attention-gated skip connections. Each block contains two $3 \times 3$ convolutions followed by Batch Normalisation and ReLU.}}{15}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Attention gate module. The gating signal $g$ from the deeper decoder level and the skip feature map $x$ are projected to a common dimension, summed, passed through ReLU, and then projected to a scalar attention coefficient $\alpha $ via sigmoid. The output is the element-wise product $\alpha \odot x$.}}{16}{figure.caption.14}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces CosineAnnealingLR versus a step-based schedule over 60 epochs. The cosine schedule (blue) decays smoothly; the step schedule (orange) drops abruptly at fixed milestones. Values are from Run~017's actual training run.}}{19}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Run~017 training curves. Left: training loss (blue) and validation loss (orange) over 60 epochs. Right: validation Dice (per-volume) over 60 epochs. The model converged smoothly with no signs of overfitting; the best validation Dice of 0.8164 (without TTA) was recorded at epoch~50.}}{20}{figure.caption.17}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Four-way TTA ensemble. Each input slice is transformed into four orientations (original, H-flip, V-flip, HV-flip). The model produces a softmax probability map for each; predictions are flipped back and averaged. The argmax of the average map is the final segmentation.}}{23}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Impact of the evaluation protocol on the reported mean Dice score. The na\"\i {}ve per-slice approach inflates the score to ${\approx }0.88$ by treating thousands of empty slices as perfect predictions. Correcting to per-volume evaluation drops the score to an honest $0.8164$ (no TTA); TTA then recovers it to $0.8769$ through genuine prediction improvement.}}{25}{figure.caption.19}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces Per-organ Dice coefficient for Run~017 (blue) versus nnU-Net~3D (orange). The proposed 2D model numerically exceeds nnU-Net~3D on 7 of 13 organs: liver, aorta, stomach, inferior vena cava, esophagus, and both adrenal glands. nnU-Net results are from a different evaluation protocol; see Table~\ref {tab:per_organ} caption for caveats.}}{27}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces Per-organ HD95 in mm for Run~017. Liver achieves a perfect HD95 of 0~mm (no boundary error detected at this voxel resolution). Spleen and right kidney show the highest HD95 values at 25--26~mm, reflecting occasional boundary offsets on a few validation volumes.}}{28}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.3}{\ignorespaces Qualitative segmentation result on FLARE22 validation case FLARE22\_Tr\_0007. Left: raw CT slice (HU windowed). Centre: ground-truth organ labels. Right: model prediction (Run~017 with TTA). Each colour corresponds to one of the 13 organ classes.}}{29}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.4}{\ignorespaces Radar chart of per-organ Dice coefficients for Run~017 (with TTA). Each spoke represents one organ; the shaded area shows the model's performance profile. Large, well-defined organs (liver, spleen, kidneys, aorta) cluster near the outer rim, while small or irregular organs (duodenum, pancreas) sit closer to the centre.}}{29}{figure.caption.25}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces Ablation results for Runs~014--017. Blue bars show Dice without TTA; orange bars show Dice with TTA. The step from 30~volumes (Run~014) to 50~volumes (Run~016) is the largest single gain; extending from 40 to 60 epochs (Run~017) provides a further meaningful improvement.}}{32}{figure.caption.27}%
